# Which preset covers which annotations, and what each one is for.
#
# Pipe separated rather than JSON, because the script that reads it is `/bin/sh` and this repository ships no
# `jq`. A JSON index would mean either a dependency an install cannot assume or a parser written in shell, and
# a parser in a Skill is the thing this whole boundary exists to avoid. Four fields, no nesting.
#
# name | document | annotations it covers | what it describes
#
# The annotation names are what a build reports as **uninterpreted**. That is how a preset is chosen: the
# Engine says which annotations it saw and did not read, and a name in this column means a preset exists for
# them. Matching on the Engine's own report rather than on a framework name is deliberate — naming the
# framework would need a list of frameworks a build knows of and cannot read, which the root PRD's section 4
# forbids by another route.
#
# Two groups are deliberately absent from the column.
#
# The mapping annotations a framework analyzer already interprets — `@RestController`, `@GetMapping`, and the
# rest — are never reported uninterpreted, so listing them would claim a preset for a question nobody asks.
#
# `@Entity`, `@Table`, `@Column`, and the other persistence mappings belong to the `jpa` preset the `nostdb`
# Skill ships. Two presets claiming one annotation would make the choice between them arbitrary.
#
# Dependency injection is absent for a third reason: this preset declares no `Bean` and no `Component`, so
# `@Service`, `@Autowired`, and `@Configuration` have no vocabulary here to offer. Claiming them would point a
# model at names that do not exist.

